steps.body:
Logical operators combine booleans.

- `a && b` is `true` only when both sides are `true`
- `a || b` is `true` when at least one side is `true`
- `!a` flips `true` to `false` and `false` to `true`

```javascript
const age = 30;
const hasId = true;
if (age >= 21 && hasId) {
  console.log("Allowed");
}
```

`!` binds tighter than `&&`, and `&&` binds tighter than `||`. Use parentheses to group a condition so it reads the way you mean: `hasId && (age < 13 || age >= 65)`.

Both operators short-circuit: they stop once the answer is known. If the left side of `&&` is `false`, or the left side of `||` is `true`, the right side never runs. That makes guards safe: in `count !== 0 && total / count > 10`, a zero `count` stops at the left side, so the division never runs. Without the guard, a positive `total` divided by 0 gives `Infinity`, and the check would pass by mistake.

Gotcha: `10 < x < 20` does not check a range. `10 < x` becomes `true` or `false` first, and that is compared with 20 as 1 or 0, so the result is always `true`. Write `x > 10 && x < 20`.

